    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d287">

                <pb facs="0482" n="386" sameAs="frus:frus1872p1/pg386"/>

                <head>No. 287.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Nelson to Mr. Fish.</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1872-02-01"><hi rendition="#italic">February</hi> 1, 1872</date>.
                        (Received February 23.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 510.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: During the last year I had frequent
                    occasion to employ my good offices with the Mexican government, on behalf of
                    English, French, and Austrian subjects, resident in this republic, in the
                    absence of a diplomatic or consular agent of either of those powers, and
                    especially <pb facs="0483" n="387" xml:id="pg387"/> in procuring for them “<hi
                        rendition="#italic">cartas de matricula,”</hi> and otherwise enabling them
                    to comply with the recent laws on the subject of foreign citizenship.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>THOMAS H. NELSON.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
